WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.270
- Thanks to CuriosityStream

00:00:01.270 --> 00:00:02.600
for keeping LegalEagle in the air

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:04.200
which now comes with Nebula for free

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.250
when you use the link in the description.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.950
Well the president has now
gone to war with Twitter

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:21.920
because Twitter is applying warning labels

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.370
to some of his tweets.

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.720
It's 2020, so that pretty much checks out.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.280
As you may know, President
Trump is a fierce critic

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:31.140
of speech that disagrees
with his own viewpoints

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.520
and don't take my word for it, take his.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:36.120
- See, I don't think
that the mainstream media

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.600
is free speech either

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.640
because it's so crooked,
it's so dishonest.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.660
So to me free speech is not
when you see something good

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:45.040
and then you purposely write bad,

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.550
to me that's very dangerous speech.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:50.130
- But as president he
believes that he has the power

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:51.590
to shut down private companies

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:53.910
who distribute comments
that he doesn't like.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:56.417
He tweeted, "Republicans feel
that social media platforms

00:00:56.417 --> 00:00:58.617
"totally silence conservative voices.

00:00:58.617 --> 00:01:01.007
"We will strongly regulate
or close them down

00:01:01.007 --> 00:01:03.610
"before we can ever allow this to happen."

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:05.120
And President Trump has toyed

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:06.620
with issuing an executive order

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:09.270
targeting online tech companies for years.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.520
But now that Twitter has
started putting warnings

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.948
on some tweets that the
President tweets out,

00:01:13.948 --> 00:01:16.880
the dam appears to have finally broken.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.010
The American President
issued an executive order

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:21.530
purporting to dictate how online platforms

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:25.020
like Twitter, Facebook and
Instagram regulate speech online,

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.660
which of course brings
up the age-old debate

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:29.840
between platforms and publishers,

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:32.410
the Communications Decency Act Section 230

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:34.170
and the First Amendment.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:36.370
So the question is what
did the President just do

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:40.655
and can he do it and does he
understand the First Amendment?

00:01:40.655 --> 00:01:43.488
(patriotic music)

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.530
Hey LegalEagles, it's time
to think like a lawyer

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:49.107
because there may not be a
single topic more misunderstood

00:01:49.107 --> 00:01:50.600
than freedom of speech.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:52.330
So let's start at the beginning.

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:55.060
The first one, the First Amendment.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:56.210
As you may know, the First Amendment

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:58.410
protects freedom of
speech among other things.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:00.610
Now the biggest misconception
about freedom of speech

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:03.010
via the First Amendment
is that it restricts

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:05.920
what the government can
do to private individuals.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:07.530
The key phrase in the First Amendment text

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.660
is that "Congress shall make no law."

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.400
This means that the First
Amendment is directed

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.720
at stopping the government
from censoring speech

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:15.320
or compelling speech.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:17.080
Private companies, generally speaking,

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:19.800
can do whatever they want
as it regards speech.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.880
Now of course there are exceptions to this

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:23.450
carved out by the Supreme Court

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:25.970
for some limited categories of obscenity

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:28.390
and creating imminent lawless action

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:30.500
but these are the tiny exceptions

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.380
to the general rule
that the First Amendment

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.410
prohibits the government from restricting

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.630
private individuals or
private companies' speech.

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:39.610
And the term freedom of speech
has a specific legal meaning.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:42.590
Freedom of speech is a negative
right against the government

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:44.462
that prevents the
government from punishing

00:02:44.462 --> 00:02:46.490
most kinds of speech.

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:48.570
Freedom of speech does not
apply to private companies,

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:49.490
generally speaking.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:51.250
And also, generally speaking,

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:54.350
it prevents the government
from forcing individuals

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:58.070
to make speech, that is
called compelled speech.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:00.827
So as a general rule, as
one commentator put it,

00:03:00.827 --> 00:03:02.097
"The Constitution protects Twitter

00:03:02.097 --> 00:03:03.307
"from the policing of a president.

00:03:03.307 --> 00:03:04.687
"It does not protect a president

00:03:04.687 --> 00:03:06.190
"from the policing of Twitter."

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:08.130
And similarly that gets into censorship

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.240
which is a different
side of the same coin.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.600
Censorship is when the
government restricts or prohibits

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:16.180
certain kinds of speech,
it's a governmental action.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:18.060
Private companies might delete comments

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:19.310
or moderate content

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:21.630
and you might think
that that's a bad thing

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:22.930
and you might have great
arguments for that,

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:25.610
but whatever that is, it's not censorship,

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:27.520
at least not in the legal sense.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.790
Nor is censorship facing the repercussions

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:31.350
of one's own speech.

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:33.010
The First Amendment and freedom of speech

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:34.947
doesn't shield you from
the social consequences

00:03:34.947 --> 00:03:37.270
of the speech that you made.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:39.220
So with that very general background

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:40.680
about the First Amendment
and freedom of speech,

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.290
that takes us to the
Communications Decency Act.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:44.600
Now you might have heard of the terms

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:46.760
platform or publisher before.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:48.930
Generally that comes
from a certain section

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:51.840
of the Communications
Decency Act Section 230.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.500
So if you've ever heard someone
refer to 230 or Section 230,

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:56.503
what they're probably talking about

00:03:56.503 --> 00:03:58.680
is the Communications Decency Act,

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.570
including the President who
just recently tweeted out

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:03.710
that we should revoke 230,
without providing any context.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:05.340
Now if you've ever read
the First Amendment,

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.700
you might notice that it
says nothing about platforms

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:11.070
or publishers or
responsibility for defamation.

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:13.100
That's because it relates to the CDA

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:15.010
which was passed in 1996.

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:17.660
Now we'll talk about what
the CDA Section 230 does

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:18.540
in just a second

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.370
but I think it's worth
going over the history

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:21.880
of the Communications Decency Act

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:23.580
because the history that led

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.450
to the founding of this particular law

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:26.850
is incredibly fascinating

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:30.135
and it harkens back to the very
beginnings of the internet.

00:04:30.135 --> 00:04:31.820
Now in the early days of the internet,

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:34.800
there were no laws regulating
the content on the internet

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:35.770
and it quickly became apparent

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:38.430
that if people posted whatever
they felt like saying,

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:40.470
some inappropriate and illegal things

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.110
could make their way onto the web.

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:43.832
Someone could post child pornography,

00:04:43.832 --> 00:04:47.250
some people could post
incredibly defamatory statements,

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:49.010
and not to mention people posting things

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:50.630
that just violate someone's copyright.

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:53.070
And lawyers and legislators had to grapple

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:55.050
with whether there would
be liability attached

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:57.720
not only to the person that
posted that information

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:00.480
but anyone who hosted that information.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:03.040
Now traditionally, content
distributors were not liable

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.880
if they distributed
something that ran afoul

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:07.140
of one of the areas where
speech is restricted

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.600
or has legal liability,

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:10.471
and I'm using the term
content distributors

00:05:10.471 --> 00:05:12.080
incredibly loosely,

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:15.490
here we're talking about
historically stores and newspapers.

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:18.010
Now in the 1950s, the
Supreme Court overturned

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:19.417
a Los Angeles ordinance that said,

00:05:19.417 --> 00:05:22.087
"If you have obscene
material in your store,

00:05:22.087 --> 00:05:24.017
"you can be held criminally responsible

00:05:24.017 --> 00:05:26.430
"for selling that paraphernalia."

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:28.900
In particular, a bookstore
owner was convicted

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:31.000
of violating the ordinance
by selling a novel

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.400
called "Sweeter than Life"
which tells the story

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:36.680
of a quote "ruthless
lesbian businesswoman."

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:38.800
The store owner was
sentenced to 30 days in jail

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:40.200
for simply selling the book.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.910
The Supreme Court said that the bookstore

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:43.640
couldn't be responsible for reviewing

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:45.910
every single thing that
was in the book or magazine

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:47.330
that it happens to sell.

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:48.630
Fast forward about 40 years

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:49.780
with the invention of the internet,

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:52.670
we now have a similar
but different situation

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:56.400
involving ISPs and
people that post content

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:57.333
on their websites.

00:05:57.333 --> 00:06:00.221
Now two of the first ISPs,
CompuServe and Prodigy,

00:06:00.221 --> 00:06:02.610
were both sued for hosting forums

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:05.090
where users posted defamatory content.

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:06.180
Prodigy, for example,

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:08.713
billed itself out as
a family-friendly ISP,

00:06:08.713 --> 00:06:12.290
so it moderated comments that
were hosted in its forums,

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:14.306
removing things that it thought were bad.

00:06:14.306 --> 00:06:16.180
Now CompuServe, on the other hand,

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:18.910
didn't moderate anything in
its forums and as a result,

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:21.730
the courts found that this
distinction was important.

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:24.460
But both CompuServe and Prodigy were sued

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:26.280
for the allegedly defamatory content

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:28.800
that was posted in
their respective forums.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:30.900
Now, the case against
CompuServe was dismissed

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:32.748
because the court
considered that CompuServe

00:06:32.748 --> 00:06:36.010
was merely a content
hoster and a distributor

00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:38.140
rather than an author or a publisher.

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:40.660
As a distributor, CompuServe
could only be held liable

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:43.000
for defamation if it knew
or had reason to know

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.580
of the defamatory nature of the content,

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:48.120
but since CompuServe wasn't
moderating its forums,

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:50.350
it had no reason to know what material

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:51.860
was posted to those forums.

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:53.890
But things turned out
differently for Prodigy,

00:06:53.890 --> 00:06:56.330
the company that wanted
to be family-friendly.

00:06:56.330 --> 00:06:58.600
A post appeared on a money talk forum

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:01.240
claiming that the investment
firm Stratton Oakmont

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:04.384
committed criminal fraud in
connection with a stock IPO.

00:07:04.384 --> 00:07:07.500
Yes, that Stratton Oakmont,

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:11.240
the one that gave rise to the
film "Wolf of Wall Street."

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:15.910
- Gentlemen, welcome to Stratton Oakmont.

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:18.070
- I can't imagine why someone thought

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:20.670
that Stratton Oakmont
was committing fraud.

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:22.030
But the firm Stratton Oakmont

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:23.930
sued not only the anonymous poster

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:26.620
who posted the allegedly
defamatory content,

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:30.250
that in retrospect was probably
not defamatory in any way,

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:33.690
but it also sued Prodigy
for hosting that content

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:35.830
and a court held that Prodigy was liable

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:38.560
as the publisher of the
content created by his users

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:41.770
since it exercised editorial
control over the messages

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:42.930
on its bulletin board.

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:45.840
And unlike CompuServe which
was compared to a bookstore,

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:47.790
Prodigy was considered to be more

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:50.450
like the editor of a
newspapers opinion page.

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:51.970
In essence, Prodigy was liable

00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:53.942
because they created content guidelines,

00:07:53.942 --> 00:07:55.980
proactively enforced those guidelines

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:59.376
and used software designed
to remove offensive language.

00:07:59.376 --> 00:08:00.760
And under this reasoning,

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:02.880
Prodigy knew or had reason to know

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:05.130
that the content was
quote unquote "defamatory"

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:07.050
since they had those guidelines in place

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:08.670
and were exercising their discretion.

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:09.503
Now it's worth noting

00:08:09.503 --> 00:08:11.269
that these weren't Supreme Court cases,

00:08:11.269 --> 00:08:13.623
these outcomes aren't necessarily dictated

00:08:13.623 --> 00:08:15.495
by the Constitution,

00:08:15.495 --> 00:08:17.670
these are lower court opinions

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:20.450
about the interaction
between ISPs and defamation.

00:08:20.450 --> 00:08:22.790
So these cases aren't
necessarily set in stone,

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:26.920
but early internet companies
drew a very clear lesson

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:27.886
from these lawsuits.

00:08:27.886 --> 00:08:30.410
They shouldn't do any moderation at all

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:33.343
since that was the best
way to avoid liability.

00:08:33.343 --> 00:08:35.090
There didn't appear to be a middle ground

00:08:35.090 --> 00:08:40.020
between absolute lawlessness
and some kind of moderation.

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:43.170
But before any of these cases
could go to the Supreme Court,

00:08:43.170 --> 00:08:44.680
Congress actually stepped in.

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:45.810
Congress didn't like the fact

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:47.350
that businesses were being held liable

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:49.160
for the acts of their users.

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:50.470
When Congress finally enacted

00:08:50.470 --> 00:08:52.110
the Communications Decency Act,

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:55.820
they were doing that in response
to the moderation dilemma.

00:08:55.820 --> 00:08:57.870
Lawmakers wanted online service providers

00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:00.020
to be able to moderate content.

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:01.530
At the time, they were especially worried

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:03.870
about things like harassment
and obscene material,

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:06.960
so they gave online providers
immunity from lawsuits

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:08.887
if they moderated their content.

00:09:08.887 --> 00:09:11.090
And as an incentive for internet companies

00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:12.106
to create basic standards,

00:09:12.106 --> 00:09:14.170
the law granted them
immunity from liability

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:16.630
if they simply adopted
standards and enforced them.

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:19.430
The Communications Decency
Act was specifically created

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:22.360
so websites didn't need to be neutral.

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:26.260
So that takes us to the
controversial Section 230.

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:28.980
The relevant portion
of the CDA Section 230

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:31.880
is actually very short,
clear, well-worded,

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:33.630
and an understandable piece of law.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:35.740
If you go to Section 230 subsection C,

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:37.770
it's called Protection for
Good Samaritan blocking

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:39.620
and screening of offensive material.

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:42.386
Sub one says "Treatment
of publisher or speaker.

00:09:42.386 --> 00:09:45.507
"No provider or user of an
interactive computer service

00:09:45.507 --> 00:09:47.714
"shall be treated as
the publisher or speaker

00:09:47.714 --> 00:09:49.487
"of any information provided

00:09:49.487 --> 00:09:52.120
"by another information content provider."

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:54.690
And the term another
information content provider

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:55.760
is incredibly important,

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:59.310
that means another user,
a commenter, a tweeter,

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:03.330
a person on Facebook, a
person posting to Instagram.

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:04.560
So that's the general immunity

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:06.100
that protects not only Twitter

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:09.220
but someone that happens
to have a YouTube channel

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:12.360
or a blog from being
liable for the postings

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:15.450
of other users and content providers.

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:17.670
But then subsection two
goes a little bit further

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:19.880
and it specifically allows for people

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:23.700
to moderate content on
their platforms or websites.

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:25.707
It states that, "No provider or user

00:10:25.707 --> 00:10:27.167
"of an interactive computer service

00:10:27.167 --> 00:10:29.367
"shall be held liable on account of A,

00:10:29.367 --> 00:10:31.377
"any action voluntarily
taken in good faith

00:10:31.377 --> 00:10:34.230
"to restrict access to or
availability of material

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:37.027
"that the provider or user
considers to be obscene,

00:10:37.027 --> 00:10:39.617
"lewd, lascivious, filthy,
excessively violent,

00:10:39.617 --> 00:10:41.527
"harassing or otherwise objectionable,

00:10:41.527 --> 00:10:42.657
"whether or not such material

00:10:42.657 --> 00:10:44.737
"is constitutionally protected or not;

00:10:44.737 --> 00:10:46.867
"or B, any action taken to enable

00:10:46.867 --> 00:10:49.817
"or make available to
information content providers

00:10:49.817 --> 00:10:52.557
"or others the technical
means to restrict access

00:10:52.557 --> 00:10:55.080
"to material described in paragraph one."

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:56.990
So let's talk about these two provisions.

00:10:56.990 --> 00:10:59.960
Section 230 C1 establishes the immunity

00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:01.330
for third party liability

00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:05.040
under which a website is not
to be treated as the speaker

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:07.370
or let's say publisher of the illegal

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:10.260
or otherwise offensive
content posted on a website

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:13.610
by a third party end user,
or what the CDA refers to

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:16.080
as another information content provider.

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:18.830
We'll summarize that as
you're not responsible

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:21.740
for what a different
user posts to your site.

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:24.390
Now section C2 also protects websites

00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:25.610
against any civil liability

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:28.290
should sites engage in self-regulation

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:30.500
or make good-faith efforts
to edit the website

00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:32.418
by blocking, screening
or restricting access

00:11:32.418 --> 00:11:35.230
to basically any content

00:11:35.230 --> 00:11:37.510
that they don't want on their website.

00:11:37.510 --> 00:11:40.400
So in other words, platforms
like Twitter, or Facebook,

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:42.231
or blogs or YouTube channels,

00:11:42.231 --> 00:11:44.420
can't be sued for defamation

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:46.250
on the basis that they had knowledge

00:11:46.250 --> 00:11:48.719
of defamatory or otherwise illegal content

00:11:48.719 --> 00:11:51.200
on the basis that they
moderated their content

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:54.380
and we're going through
comments or other postings

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:56.070
and removing ones that they didn't like,

00:11:56.070 --> 00:11:57.600
or didn't want on their website.

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:00.760
And it's hard to overstate
the importance of Section 230

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:02.500
in the development of the early internet,

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:05.490
and really as the internet
has developed to this day.

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:07.400
Whether you agree with it or not,

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:10.910
it is a powerful tool
that provides immunity

00:12:10.910 --> 00:12:13.670
and shields internet service
providers and websites

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:16.380
from liability for content that they host,

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:18.750
and we'll certainly get
into the differing opinions

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:20.830
on whether 230 is a good
thing or a bad thing.

00:12:20.830 --> 00:12:23.230
But as a legal matter,
decades of court decisions

00:12:23.230 --> 00:12:24.950
have affirmed the robust immunity

00:12:24.950 --> 00:12:27.810
that is provided under
the CDA Section 230.

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:29.370
And here are a few recent examples

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:31.650
that show how Section 230 has been used

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:33.940
as a shield by content providers.

00:12:33.940 --> 00:12:36.320
For example, a man sued
the Grindr dating app

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:37.960
for negligence, products liability,

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:40.130
and related claims after
someone impersonated him

00:12:40.130 --> 00:12:42.350
and posted fake profiles on the app.

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:43.840
The court found that Section 230

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:46.270
barred claims against Grindr
for the design of its platform

00:12:46.270 --> 00:12:47.714
as within the purview of protected

00:12:47.714 --> 00:12:49.630
traditional editorial functions.

00:12:49.630 --> 00:12:51.300
Similarly, in Wisconsin a plaintiff

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:53.010
sued a classified advertising website

00:12:53.010 --> 00:12:54.364
because the plaintiff wound up buying

00:12:54.364 --> 00:12:57.820
what turned out to be a murder
weapon from a private seller.

00:12:57.820 --> 00:12:58.890
The case was dismissed

00:12:58.890 --> 00:13:00.096
because the plaintiff sued the website

00:13:00.096 --> 00:13:02.410
claiming that the website was responsible

00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:04.680
for what someone else
had posted to the website

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:07.080
and ultimately sold to that person.

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:08.780
But the website didn't sell the gun,

00:13:08.780 --> 00:13:10.790
it just hosted the ad for it.

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:12.951
So it wasn't responsible for what happened

00:13:12.951 --> 00:13:14.890
to go wrong with that sale.

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:17.700
But here's a key point
that's often misunderstood.

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:20.800
In both of those cases, the
person that wrote the posts

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:22.900
that ultimately was misleading or illegal,

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:25.002
they're responsible for
the legal repercussions

00:13:25.002 --> 00:13:26.285
of those posts,

00:13:26.285 --> 00:13:29.400
just like anyone that posts
defamatory content on Twitter

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:32.340
is responsible for the defamation
that they may have done.

00:13:32.340 --> 00:13:35.160
The only thing that the
CDA Section 230 does

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:38.063
is it exempts the
platform, or the website,

00:13:38.063 --> 00:13:40.040
from being legally responsible

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:42.970
for the posting of some other
third-party content user.

00:13:42.970 --> 00:13:45.673
But by the same token,
websites and platforms

00:13:45.673 --> 00:13:47.300
want the flexibility to be able

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:50.060
to remove those kinds of
postings if they so choose.

00:13:50.060 --> 00:13:51.390
If they find out that someone

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:54.540
is trying to sell a murder
weapon in a classified ad,

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:57.680
they want the flexibility to
be able to remove that post

00:13:57.680 --> 00:13:58.740
if they find out about it.

00:13:58.740 --> 00:14:00.720
So if you've ever wondered why websites

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:03.600
have these incredibly long
terms of service contracts

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:06.370
that effectively allow websites
to do whatever they want

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:08.960
and remove whatever content
or users they want to,

00:14:08.960 --> 00:14:09.793
that's why.

00:14:09.793 --> 00:14:12.080
But obviously Section 230
has received criticism

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:14.030
on both the left and the right.

00:14:14.030 --> 00:14:16.300
There are many people who
claim that Section 230

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:19.230
discriminates against
certain kinds of viewpoints,

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:21.020
in particular, political viewpoints,

00:14:21.020 --> 00:14:23.410
and ironically my Twitter feed
is full of people on the left

00:14:23.410 --> 00:14:25.970
who claim that tech companies
are biased against their views

00:14:25.970 --> 00:14:27.820
and people on the right who
claim that tech companies

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:30.010
are biased against their views as well.

00:14:30.010 --> 00:14:31.450
Certainly it's not surprising

00:14:31.450 --> 00:14:35.180
that when websites moderate
their content and their users,

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:37.160
that people are going to claim viewpoint

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:38.730
in political viewpoint discrimination.

00:14:38.730 --> 00:14:41.130
But as a practical matter,
the vast majority of people

00:14:41.130 --> 00:14:44.210
probably don't want
true neutral platforms.

00:14:44.210 --> 00:14:47.361
It's a place of anarchy
that's ruled by extremists

00:14:47.361 --> 00:14:49.370
and the hecklers.

00:14:49.370 --> 00:14:51.120
There's this concept
in First Amendment law

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:53.412
called the heckler's
veto where, for example,

00:14:53.412 --> 00:14:55.610
you're not allowed to discriminate

00:14:55.610 --> 00:14:58.570
against a speaker coming
to let's say a public forum

00:14:58.570 --> 00:15:00.990
based on the reaction of the people

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:02.950
that are going to view that speech.

00:15:02.950 --> 00:15:04.890
So for example, if there
was gonna be a speaker

00:15:04.890 --> 00:15:07.800
at a college campus and you
knew that the college kids

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:10.630
were going to riots or create violence

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:12.670
in response to this particular speaker,

00:15:12.670 --> 00:15:15.470
if you allowed that violence to dictate

00:15:15.470 --> 00:15:17.850
who can and cannot come
to the college campus,

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:21.210
the hecklers are allowed to
veto any kinds of speakers,

00:15:21.210 --> 00:15:23.870
and so that's not a valid basis

00:15:23.870 --> 00:15:25.940
to discriminate against
someone's viewpoint

00:15:25.940 --> 00:15:27.410
in a public forum, that again,

00:15:27.410 --> 00:15:29.720
has nothing to do with a private forum.

00:15:29.720 --> 00:15:31.510
But you can imagine that
there's an analogy there

00:15:31.510 --> 00:15:34.470
to where if you're not allowed
to do content moderation,

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:36.470
that is the extremists
and the loudest voices

00:15:36.470 --> 00:15:38.880
that are going to rule all
of these different platforms.

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:42.183
Now there's a time and a
place for a 4chan or an 8chan,

00:15:42.183 --> 00:15:44.680
I just don't want to hang out there

00:15:44.680 --> 00:15:46.660
and I don't think most people do either.

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:50.330
And the ultimate irony is that
if Section 230 didn't exist

00:15:50.330 --> 00:15:52.860
or it was revoked, and Twitter was liable

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:54.950
for anything that its users posted,

00:15:54.950 --> 00:15:58.750
as a result they 100% would
delete Donald Trump's account

00:15:58.750 --> 00:16:00.580
and would not allow him to post

00:16:00.580 --> 00:16:02.330
or they would vet all of his tweets

00:16:02.330 --> 00:16:04.610
before allowing them to go public.

00:16:04.610 --> 00:16:06.610
And we focus on the big tech companies

00:16:06.610 --> 00:16:08.520
but it goes far, far deeper than that,

00:16:08.520 --> 00:16:10.810
it's every YouTube channel, every blog,

00:16:10.810 --> 00:16:12.750
any website that hosts comments.

00:16:12.750 --> 00:16:14.920
I asked my fellow internet
lawyer Richard Hoeg

00:16:14.920 --> 00:16:17.200
to talk about what would
happen if Section 230

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:19.440
were revoked or severely curtailed.

00:16:19.440 --> 00:16:21.400
- Hey LegalEagle, it's Rick from Hoeg Law,

00:16:21.400 --> 00:16:23.380
you might remember me
from such YouTube videos

00:16:23.380 --> 00:16:25.420
as COPPA, I just can't quit you;

00:16:25.420 --> 00:16:29.650
COPPA, it's not you, it's
me; and other things.

00:16:29.650 --> 00:16:31.960
As you rightly point out, CDA Section 230

00:16:31.960 --> 00:16:34.590
is a foundational piece of
the internet superstructure

00:16:34.590 --> 00:16:36.310
and as a foundational piece,

00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:37.870
it's important not just to the Apple,

00:16:37.870 --> 00:16:39.439
Facebooks and Googles of the world,

00:16:39.439 --> 00:16:41.430
but also to places like ResetEra

00:16:41.430 --> 00:16:43.350
if you're interested in
talking about video games

00:16:43.350 --> 00:16:47.180
or Reddit if you're interested
in talking about video games.

00:16:47.180 --> 00:16:49.590
The language of Section
230 makes no distinction

00:16:49.590 --> 00:16:51.600
between large operators and small

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:53.820
which means it protects everyone.

00:16:53.820 --> 00:16:58.080
But also that a change in
that law will affect everyone,

00:16:58.080 --> 00:16:59.740
and not to put too fine a point on it,

00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:02.546
but the FTC most recently,
while discussing COPPA,

00:17:02.546 --> 00:17:06.870
has intimated that YouTube
channel runners like you or me

00:17:06.870 --> 00:17:08.430
are responsible for the content

00:17:08.430 --> 00:17:09.850
that appears on those channels

00:17:09.850 --> 00:17:12.160
as if they were YouTube itself.

00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:15.410
So what does removal of Section 230 mean?

00:17:15.410 --> 00:17:18.470
It means that places like
Reddit or your channel or mine

00:17:18.470 --> 00:17:20.730
might have to be responsible
for every comment

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:22.250
that appears on them.

00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:23.570
You might have to be responsible

00:17:23.570 --> 00:17:25.950
for the comments that appear
on your YouTube videos,

00:17:25.950 --> 00:17:28.460
I don't want that, I
know you don't either.

00:17:28.460 --> 00:17:30.461
Now I'm not trying to
pretend that Section 230

00:17:30.461 --> 00:17:34.250
or DMCA or COPPA
descended from divine writ

00:17:34.250 --> 00:17:36.285
and that we need to hold fast to every bit

00:17:36.285 --> 00:17:37.947
of the language that was offered,

00:17:37.947 --> 00:17:41.310
but I do think that we
need to be very cautious

00:17:41.310 --> 00:17:45.370
about any law that
affects this many people.

00:17:45.370 --> 00:17:46.430
- I think Richard's right.

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:49.170
The stakes are incredibly
high and revoking 230

00:17:49.170 --> 00:17:51.340
could destroy the internet as we know it.

00:17:51.340 --> 00:17:53.050
Definitely check out
Richard's YouTube channel

00:17:53.050 --> 00:17:54.900
that has great analysis about internet

00:17:54.900 --> 00:17:56.149
and entertainment law.

00:17:56.149 --> 00:17:59.060
So that's the basics about
the Communications Decency Act

00:17:59.060 --> 00:18:01.730
but misunderstandings about Section 230

00:18:01.730 --> 00:18:02.980
abound on the internet,

00:18:02.980 --> 00:18:05.480
so let's take some time to dispel

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:07.130
some of those misunderstandings.

00:18:07.130 --> 00:18:10.530
The first is of course what
about publishers and platforms?

00:18:10.530 --> 00:18:12.714
Journalists, politicians
and internet commentators

00:18:12.714 --> 00:18:14.320
frequently make a mistake

00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:17.010
when they talk about CTA
Section 230 immunity.

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:19.950
They often say that these
internet companies or websites

00:18:19.950 --> 00:18:21.210
either are publishers

00:18:21.210 --> 00:18:23.850
and are responsible for the
content that their users post

00:18:23.850 --> 00:18:26.260
or that they should be
treated like publishers

00:18:26.260 --> 00:18:28.800
and held responsible for
what their users post

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:31.590
regardless of what the law
currently is constituted.

00:18:31.590 --> 00:18:33.890
But that's not really the key distinction.

00:18:33.890 --> 00:18:35.560
Websites are already liable

00:18:35.560 --> 00:18:37.720
for the content that they create.

00:18:37.720 --> 00:18:40.120
A website like Twitter, or Facebook,

00:18:40.120 --> 00:18:42.320
or a newspaper like "The New York Times"

00:18:42.320 --> 00:18:46.750
can and often does create
contents and when they do that,

00:18:46.750 --> 00:18:50.340
they are responsible for
the legal repercussions

00:18:50.340 --> 00:18:52.320
of the content that they create.

00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:55.404
A website can be both
a publisher, an author,

00:18:55.404 --> 00:18:58.180
and a platform at the same time.

00:18:58.180 --> 00:19:00.360
As the law stands, they
just aren't responsible

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:03.580
for the content of the
users or other third parties

00:19:03.580 --> 00:19:05.850
that post to those websites or platforms

00:19:05.850 --> 00:19:07.900
nor are they liable if they choose

00:19:07.900 --> 00:19:10.030
to moderate their users' content.

00:19:10.030 --> 00:19:13.170
The law currently recognizes
that some internet providers

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:16.080
share some similarities to
edited print publications

00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:18.034
like newspapers in certain contexts,

00:19:18.034 --> 00:19:20.060
which are, under some circumstances,

00:19:20.060 --> 00:19:21.730
held liable for third party content.

00:19:21.730 --> 00:19:24.310
However, they also have
attributes of common carriers

00:19:24.310 --> 00:19:25.235
like telephone companies

00:19:25.235 --> 00:19:27.720
which serve as passive distributors

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:29.030
of third-party information.

00:19:29.030 --> 00:19:31.620
Under Section 230, websites
are held responsible

00:19:31.620 --> 00:19:33.830
for the content that
they create themselves

00:19:33.830 --> 00:19:35.246
but not for the content of other people

00:19:35.246 --> 00:19:37.240
that's simply posted.

00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:39.760
So this division between publisher,

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:42.862
platform or distributor
is kind of a red herring.

00:19:42.862 --> 00:19:45.130
And the terms platform and publisher

00:19:45.130 --> 00:19:46.810
get very, very confused.

00:19:46.810 --> 00:19:48.660
Really, when you think
of this distinction,

00:19:48.660 --> 00:19:50.150
you should think of it as the distinction

00:19:50.150 --> 00:19:52.420
between a host and an author.

00:19:52.420 --> 00:19:55.070
Hosts aren't liable
for other users content

00:19:55.070 --> 00:19:57.490
but authors are always responsible

00:19:57.490 --> 00:19:59.420
for the content that they author.

00:19:59.420 --> 00:20:01.520
Sometimes a website can be an author,

00:20:01.520 --> 00:20:04.950
and sometimes a website can
host other people's content,

00:20:04.950 --> 00:20:07.510
and sometimes a website can
do both at the same time,

00:20:07.510 --> 00:20:09.970
you have to look at the
particular speech in question

00:20:09.970 --> 00:20:12.540
to determine what
applies in which context.

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:14.220
And there are two other concepts

00:20:14.220 --> 00:20:16.580
that are continually
made part of this debate

00:20:16.580 --> 00:20:17.720
that sort of get lumped into it.

00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:20.440
One is neutrality and the
other is the Fairness Doctrine.

00:20:20.440 --> 00:20:22.260
Now neutrality can mean different things

00:20:22.260 --> 00:20:24.119
in different contexts but in this context,

00:20:24.119 --> 00:20:26.910
Senator Josh Hawley has
introduced legislation

00:20:26.910 --> 00:20:28.984
that would amend the CDA Section 230

00:20:28.984 --> 00:20:30.330
so that internet companies

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:32.247
would lose their Section 230 immunity

00:20:32.247 --> 00:20:34.130
if they did not apply their rules

00:20:34.130 --> 00:20:36.250
in a politically neutral manner.

00:20:36.250 --> 00:20:37.770
This is obviously a response

00:20:37.770 --> 00:20:40.450
to the perceived anti-right-wing bias

00:20:40.450 --> 00:20:42.000
of certain tech companies.

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:44.820
This is one of many amendments
that are on the table

00:20:44.820 --> 00:20:47.890
that haven't passed but
potentially could in the future.

00:20:47.890 --> 00:20:50.718
Query whether this sort of
viewpoint discrimination,

00:20:50.718 --> 00:20:53.193
in other words, not allowing
viewpoint discrimination

00:20:53.193 --> 00:20:55.010
passes First Amendment muster

00:20:55.010 --> 00:20:57.512
because the government
generally can't mandate

00:20:57.512 --> 00:21:01.000
that certain viewpoints
be allowed or disallowed.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:02.840
The First Amendment has
been around for a long time

00:21:02.840 --> 00:21:04.190
and the Communications Decency Act

00:21:04.190 --> 00:21:05.660
has been around since 1996,

00:21:05.660 --> 00:21:10.610
so to take this action to
change it is anti-conservative

00:21:10.610 --> 00:21:13.700
because you're changing the status quo.

00:21:13.700 --> 00:21:15.910
But that's a question for another time.

00:21:15.910 --> 00:21:18.930
Now the related concept
of the Fairness Doctrine

00:21:18.930 --> 00:21:22.530
harkens back to the
days of broadcast media

00:21:22.530 --> 00:21:23.910
like radio and TV

00:21:23.910 --> 00:21:27.030
which once had a legal duty
to air balanced content

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:30.360
covering both sides of a
politically controversial issue.

00:21:30.360 --> 00:21:32.080
This was called the Fairness Doctrine.

00:21:32.080 --> 00:21:34.310
The idea was that because you were using

00:21:34.310 --> 00:21:35.470
the broadcast spectrum

00:21:35.470 --> 00:21:37.850
of which there was only a limited supply,

00:21:37.850 --> 00:21:41.440
the broadcast networks had a
duty to present both sides.

00:21:41.440 --> 00:21:43.083
Now Republicans fought for years

00:21:43.083 --> 00:21:44.740
against the Fairness Doctrine

00:21:44.740 --> 00:21:46.650
because they argued that
the balance requirement

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:48.132
had a chilling effect on the delivery

00:21:48.132 --> 00:21:51.540
of the conservative message,
and finally in 1987,

00:21:51.540 --> 00:21:54.110
President Reagan repealed
the Fairness Doctrine.

00:21:54.110 --> 00:21:56.640
This was considered a huge
victory for freedom of speech

00:21:56.640 --> 00:21:59.440
because broadcast networks
were no longer mandated

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:00.960
to carry certain messages

00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:03.220
and it actually led to the flourishing

00:22:03.220 --> 00:22:04.623
of things like conservative talk radio

00:22:04.623 --> 00:22:07.160
since stations were
free to broadcast views

00:22:07.160 --> 00:22:09.550
without offering other sides of the story.

00:22:09.550 --> 00:22:11.490
And while the original Fairness Doctrine

00:22:11.490 --> 00:22:13.694
only applied to broadcast media,

00:22:13.694 --> 00:22:15.690
those generally on the right

00:22:15.690 --> 00:22:17.790
who are detractors of the current makeup

00:22:17.790 --> 00:22:19.620
of the Communications Decency Act

00:22:19.620 --> 00:22:22.320
want to basically instill
a new Fairness Doctrine

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:27.100
onto the public platforms
that hosts users content.

00:22:27.100 --> 00:22:29.350
So with that context,
that brings us to the war

00:22:29.350 --> 00:22:32.240
between President Trump
and Twitter's Jack Dorsey.

00:22:32.240 --> 00:22:33.170
During the last few weeks,

00:22:33.170 --> 00:22:35.370
the president has gotten
into several Twitter wars,

00:22:35.370 --> 00:22:37.800
first calling MSNBC's Joe Scarborough

00:22:37.800 --> 00:22:39.857
a murderer of a former staff member,

00:22:39.857 --> 00:22:42.450
prompting both Scarborough
and the family of the deceased

00:22:42.450 --> 00:22:44.420
to speak out against Trump's comments.

00:22:44.420 --> 00:22:49.420
- Vile people driven by
hatred and petty politics.

00:22:49.740 --> 00:22:51.810
- Though ironically,
since Trump's comments

00:22:51.810 --> 00:22:54.600
could get him sued for
defamation when he leaves office,

00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:56.390
Twitter probably would not be responsible

00:22:56.390 --> 00:22:58.680
'cause they're immune under
the Communications Decency Act.

00:22:58.680 --> 00:23:00.530
Second, the president
has been bashing states

00:23:00.530 --> 00:23:03.200
that are promoting voting
by mail during a pandemic

00:23:03.200 --> 00:23:04.740
and as part of this feud,

00:23:04.740 --> 00:23:07.770
the president has made
some wild statements

00:23:07.770 --> 00:23:10.410
about predictions of voter fraud

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:13.251
that would come as a
result of voting by mail

00:23:13.251 --> 00:23:16.420
which are most likely false.

00:23:16.420 --> 00:23:18.170
Now after considerable public pressure,

00:23:18.170 --> 00:23:20.220
Twitter decided not to moderate the tweets

00:23:20.220 --> 00:23:24.300
about the alleged murder
but did add some context

00:23:24.300 --> 00:23:26.000
to the tweets about voter fraud,

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:28.170
and then recently President
Trump spent some time

00:23:28.170 --> 00:23:30.260
rage tweeting about
racial tensions in the US

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:32.480
threatening to sic the
military on protesters

00:23:32.480 --> 00:23:36.200
and effectively threatening
to shoot looters.

00:23:36.200 --> 00:23:38.990
Twitter labeled those
tweets as promoting violence

00:23:38.990 --> 00:23:41.196
and added a warning gate
that you had to go through

00:23:41.196 --> 00:23:44.020
before you could actually
view the President's tweet.

00:23:44.020 --> 00:23:45.500
And however you may feel

00:23:45.500 --> 00:23:47.330
about the President's underlying tweets

00:23:47.330 --> 00:23:48.320
or Twitter's response,

00:23:48.320 --> 00:23:51.790
it's more or less
Twitter's decision to make

00:23:51.790 --> 00:23:56.210
and I would argue that
the language of the labels

00:23:56.210 --> 00:23:58.380
that they added to these tweets

00:23:58.380 --> 00:24:01.460
would constitute something
that was authored by Twitter.

00:24:01.460 --> 00:24:05.375
So the language of the
labels that were applied,

00:24:05.375 --> 00:24:07.940
if for example, those are defamatory,

00:24:07.940 --> 00:24:09.370
which I don't think people are arguing,

00:24:09.370 --> 00:24:12.130
but if they were, then
Twitter would be liable

00:24:12.130 --> 00:24:13.760
for a defamatory statement

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:16.910
because Twitter did in fact
author those particular tweets.

00:24:16.910 --> 00:24:19.030
But that's a statement by Twitter itself

00:24:19.030 --> 00:24:22.430
as opposed to the
statement of a third party.

00:24:22.430 --> 00:24:24.680
But regardless, this action by Twitter

00:24:24.680 --> 00:24:26.630
pushed President Trump over the edge,

00:24:26.630 --> 00:24:29.100
leading to the long
threatened executive order

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:32.610
against social media platforms
that he doesn't agree with.

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:36.020
So let's look at the executive
order of the President.

00:24:36.020 --> 00:24:39.410
Now remember, an executive
order can't change the law.

00:24:39.410 --> 00:24:41.740
Now sometimes an executive
order can be very powerful

00:24:41.740 --> 00:24:43.630
because the President is in control

00:24:43.630 --> 00:24:45.718
of the entire executive branch
which includes the military

00:24:45.718 --> 00:24:47.680
and the entire administrative state,

00:24:47.680 --> 00:24:50.550
but where Congress has
written something into law,

00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:53.040
a presidential executive
order cannot change

00:24:53.040 --> 00:24:54.231
what Congress has done,

00:24:54.231 --> 00:24:56.990
but that hasn't stopped
the President from trying.

00:24:56.990 --> 00:24:58.670
And this particular executive order

00:24:58.670 --> 00:25:00.750
goes straight at Section 230

00:25:00.750 --> 00:25:02.780
of the Communications Decency Act.

00:25:02.780 --> 00:25:05.300
The executive order starts
by making policy arguments

00:25:05.300 --> 00:25:07.880
about what it's trying to accomplish

00:25:07.880 --> 00:25:09.250
and make some statements

00:25:09.250 --> 00:25:11.330
about freedom of speech
in the First Amendment

00:25:11.330 --> 00:25:13.350
and remember, when we started this video

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:15.670
that this largely is
not a free speech issue,

00:25:15.670 --> 00:25:18.950
this is speech that comes
from a private party,

00:25:18.950 --> 00:25:21.470
freedom of speech relates
to a negative right

00:25:21.470 --> 00:25:22.400
against the government.

00:25:22.400 --> 00:25:24.920
So for the most part,
makes the classic mistake

00:25:24.920 --> 00:25:26.340
of talking about freedom of speech

00:25:26.340 --> 00:25:29.900
when we're talking about the
speech of private individuals.

00:25:29.900 --> 00:25:32.070
Now the executive order goes on to attack

00:25:32.070 --> 00:25:35.530
the good faith requirement
of the moderation of websites

00:25:35.530 --> 00:25:38.170
and says that because effectively websites

00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:40.540
are making viewpoint discriminations

00:25:40.540 --> 00:25:43.270
related to political ideals,

00:25:43.270 --> 00:25:45.480
that they're not operating in good faith.

00:25:45.480 --> 00:25:49.270
Now that is the President's
opinion about what the law says,

00:25:49.270 --> 00:25:51.360
an interpretation of good faith,

00:25:51.360 --> 00:25:54.740
that is not how courts have
interpreted that requirement

00:25:54.740 --> 00:25:57.370
and that's not how courts
have made requirements

00:25:57.370 --> 00:25:59.810
for websites that actually moderate

00:25:59.810 --> 00:26:00.840
their particular comments.

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:03.130
So this executive order does not carry

00:26:03.130 --> 00:26:05.970
the effect of law with
respect to what courts

00:26:05.970 --> 00:26:09.750
are going to assume about Section 230

00:26:09.750 --> 00:26:11.490
of the Communications Decency Act

00:26:11.490 --> 00:26:13.140
and it's possible that Congress

00:26:13.140 --> 00:26:16.455
could change that requirement
and adopt the Holly Amendment

00:26:16.455 --> 00:26:17.974
but at the moment, they haven't,

00:26:17.974 --> 00:26:20.070
so this portion of the executive order

00:26:20.070 --> 00:26:21.242
doesn't really accomplish anything.

00:26:21.242 --> 00:26:23.926
But the order does go on to
say that all federal agencies

00:26:23.926 --> 00:26:26.670
should adopt the President's understanding

00:26:26.670 --> 00:26:30.420
of this requirement and
effectively curtail Section 230

00:26:30.420 --> 00:26:33.210
and it asks the Department
of Commerce and the FCC

00:26:33.210 --> 00:26:36.267
to adopt rulemaking to
effectuate that decision.

00:26:36.267 --> 00:26:39.459
The FCC is supposed to be an
independent regulatory body,

00:26:39.459 --> 00:26:42.760
so it's not clear whether
they will adopt this or not,

00:26:42.760 --> 00:26:44.270
and generally the FCC has said

00:26:44.270 --> 00:26:45.550
it actually doesn't have the power

00:26:45.550 --> 00:26:47.080
to police the internet in this way.

00:26:47.080 --> 00:26:49.500
So we'll see what happens
with respect to that.

00:26:49.500 --> 00:26:51.404
The executive order also asked the FTC

00:26:51.404 --> 00:26:54.695
to consider website moderation
and unfair trade practice

00:26:54.695 --> 00:26:57.250
which might result in some action,

00:26:57.250 --> 00:27:00.470
but I have to believe that
if the FTC punished websites

00:27:00.470 --> 00:27:01.728
for viewpoint discrimination,

00:27:01.728 --> 00:27:02.829
they'd run headlong

00:27:02.829 --> 00:27:05.220
into a bunch of First Amendment lawsuits.

00:27:05.220 --> 00:27:08.800
So not clear how strong
that action would be.

00:27:08.800 --> 00:27:12.210
It also purports to create a
mechanism for public complaints

00:27:12.210 --> 00:27:15.150
about violations of
neutrality in moderation

00:27:15.150 --> 00:27:16.738
and asks for a working group

00:27:16.738 --> 00:27:19.320
related to state attorneys general

00:27:19.320 --> 00:27:21.960
to be able to view state laws

00:27:21.960 --> 00:27:24.850
to prevent this kind of moderation,

00:27:24.850 --> 00:27:27.700
though traditionally the
Communications Decency Act

00:27:27.700 --> 00:27:31.390
has been ruled to preempt
state laws as well,

00:27:31.390 --> 00:27:36.390
so it's not clear what, if
any, the state AG's can do

00:27:37.810 --> 00:27:39.430
in this particular context.

00:27:39.430 --> 00:27:41.870
Now interestingly, the
executive order also relies

00:27:41.870 --> 00:27:43.950
on a legal theory that internet platforms

00:27:43.950 --> 00:27:46.790
are the functional modern
equivalent of a public forum

00:27:46.790 --> 00:27:49.060
and therefore it's everyone's right

00:27:49.060 --> 00:27:51.520
to be able to access that public forum.

00:27:51.520 --> 00:27:53.700
The theory is that the
importance of social media

00:27:53.700 --> 00:27:55.730
in public discourse means
that social media sites

00:27:55.730 --> 00:27:58.110
should be treated as government actors

00:27:58.110 --> 00:27:59.800
subject to the First Amendment

00:27:59.800 --> 00:28:01.930
rather than as private entities.

00:28:01.930 --> 00:28:04.330
And if websites were
treated as state actors,

00:28:04.330 --> 00:28:06.400
then they would need to
follow the First Amendment

00:28:06.400 --> 00:28:07.810
just as the government does

00:28:07.810 --> 00:28:09.140
and just like the government,

00:28:09.140 --> 00:28:11.184
they couldn't ban, block or delete content

00:28:11.184 --> 00:28:14.420
based on the viewpoints of
that particular content.

00:28:14.420 --> 00:28:17.120
This of course is the
antithesis of Section 230

00:28:17.120 --> 00:28:19.900
which encouraged private
companies to moderate content

00:28:19.900 --> 00:28:22.950
rather than let the internet
collapse into anarchy.

00:28:22.950 --> 00:28:24.500
But at the heart of this debate

00:28:24.500 --> 00:28:28.710
is whether websites
like Twitter or Facebook

00:28:28.710 --> 00:28:31.793
are the functional
equivalent of a town square

00:28:31.793 --> 00:28:34.230
or whether they're like a private house.

00:28:34.230 --> 00:28:37.590
Now a town square, generally
has been a physical location

00:28:37.590 --> 00:28:41.040
that generally was a public
good, publicly owned,

00:28:41.040 --> 00:28:43.270
and thus you couldn't restrict people

00:28:43.270 --> 00:28:45.040
from being able to access it.

00:28:45.040 --> 00:28:48.400
Whereas a private house,
when you own that house,

00:28:48.400 --> 00:28:51.640
you can restrict as
much speech as you want,

00:28:51.640 --> 00:28:53.430
so it's a question of that.

00:28:53.430 --> 00:28:57.174
And there are some cases that
in very, very limited contexts

00:28:57.174 --> 00:28:59.949
have applied the sort
of town square analogy

00:28:59.949 --> 00:29:01.950
to private places.

00:29:01.950 --> 00:29:05.042
For example, certain
malls and company towns

00:29:05.042 --> 00:29:10.042
that were more like a traditional
physical public location,

00:29:11.709 --> 00:29:14.080
have under some circumstances

00:29:14.080 --> 00:29:16.640
had those restrictions applied to them,

00:29:16.640 --> 00:29:19.082
but in the vast majority of cases

00:29:19.082 --> 00:29:23.120
where you have a private
company or a private individual

00:29:23.120 --> 00:29:27.600
whether it's a restaurant,
or a commercial building,

00:29:27.600 --> 00:29:29.220
or your own private house,

00:29:29.220 --> 00:29:31.290
you're not bound by the First Amendment

00:29:31.290 --> 00:29:33.810
and you can restrict or promote
whatever speech you want

00:29:33.810 --> 00:29:36.290
within the confines of
your own private property.

00:29:36.290 --> 00:29:38.191
And sometimes those lines get blurred,

00:29:38.191 --> 00:29:40.330
there are no easy questions in law.

00:29:40.330 --> 00:29:44.800
So for example, when a
government entity rents a hall

00:29:44.800 --> 00:29:46.220
for a public forum,

00:29:46.220 --> 00:29:48.470
they can't engage in
viewpoint discrimination

00:29:48.470 --> 00:29:49.920
even though it's private property,

00:29:49.920 --> 00:29:52.100
but it's sort of been
commandeered by the government,

00:29:52.100 --> 00:29:56.040
or when a governmental
official uses Twitter

00:29:56.040 --> 00:29:59.260
as the official mouthpiece
of that government agency,

00:29:59.260 --> 00:30:00.728
there have been a few cases that have said

00:30:00.728 --> 00:30:04.060
you're more like a government
than a private entity

00:30:04.060 --> 00:30:08.269
and that's in this particular
limited channel or username.

00:30:08.269 --> 00:30:09.711
But it's certainly been interesting to see

00:30:09.711 --> 00:30:12.744
not only Twitter's response
to the President's tweets

00:30:12.744 --> 00:30:16.300
but the President's response
to Twitter's response.

00:30:16.300 --> 00:30:18.590
- The choices that Twitter
makes when it chooses

00:30:18.590 --> 00:30:22.980
to suppress, edit, blacklist, shadow, ban

00:30:22.980 --> 00:30:25.380
are editorial decisions, pure and simple,

00:30:25.380 --> 00:30:27.210
they're editorial decisions.

00:30:27.210 --> 00:30:30.541
- After having some of his
tweets be warning gated,

00:30:30.541 --> 00:30:33.890
the President apparently
backed down a little bit

00:30:33.890 --> 00:30:36.190
and reversed course on
some of the statements

00:30:36.190 --> 00:30:38.770
that he was making and you
know lost in this whole shuffle

00:30:38.770 --> 00:30:41.450
is that Twitter actually
has a First Amendment right

00:30:41.450 --> 00:30:44.870
to express its opinion about what Trump

00:30:44.870 --> 00:30:47.190
is expressing in his tweets.

00:30:47.190 --> 00:30:50.410
Now like any individual
or company or person,

00:30:50.410 --> 00:30:52.587
Twitter will be
responsible for the content

00:30:52.587 --> 00:30:54.700
that they actually author,

00:30:54.700 --> 00:30:57.680
but it is of course related
to this larger context

00:30:57.680 --> 00:31:01.050
about content moderation
on websites and platforms

00:31:01.050 --> 00:31:04.010
and when a website is responsible
for that content and not.

00:31:04.010 --> 00:31:06.500
And there are certainly
advantages and disadvantages

00:31:06.500 --> 00:31:09.040
to having Section 230 on the books.

00:31:09.040 --> 00:31:12.340
It is largely responsible for
how the internet looks today

00:31:12.340 --> 00:31:13.970
whether you like that or not.

00:31:13.970 --> 00:31:15.730
And while it's probably a bad idea

00:31:15.730 --> 00:31:17.860
to repeal Section 230 entirely,

00:31:17.860 --> 00:31:20.310
what we really need is a
law that prevents YouTubers

00:31:20.310 --> 00:31:23.200
who give great legal analysis
from getting demonetized

00:31:23.200 --> 00:31:25.030
when they talk about sensitive subjects.

